  $post$
  <p>Most candidates searching for free full-length IELTS practice exams hit the same wall: incomplete section samples, broken audio links, sketchy third-party PDFs that look nothing like the real test, and sites that call something a "mock exam" when it's really just 10 reading questions and a timer. It's a genuine waste of study time when you're already under pressure.</p>
  <p>So where can you find free full-length IELTS practice exams that are actually worth sitting? This article cuts through the noise. Below are 7 specific places where you can access complete, four-skill IELTS practice exams for free, along with exactly what each source gives you and where its limits are. One of these options goes beyond handing you a test paper, it uses AI to score your Writing and Speaking the moment you finish and gives you criterion-referenced feedback before you close the tab.</p>
  <h2>Where can I find free full-length IELTS practice exams? Start with these 3 official sources</h2>
  <p>If format accuracy matters to you (and it should), start here. These are the only sources where the question format, timing, and task types are guaranteed to match the real exam, because they're produced by the organisations that own IELTS.</p>
  <h3>1. IELTS.org and Cambridge: the gold-standard download</h3>
  <p><a href="https://ielts.org/take-a-test/preparation-resources/sample-test-questions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IELTS.org publishes free sample test materials</a> for both Academic and General Training. The Listening section comes with a downloadable PDF question paper, a blank answer sheet, MP3 audio files, and full transcripts. Reading and Writing sample tasks are also available as PDFs with answer sheets. These aren't timed full mocks in a browser; they're offline practice papers you use on your own schedule, and they're the most trustworthy format reference you'll find at no cost.</p>
  <h3>2. British Council's familiarisation test materials</h3>
  <p>The <a href="https://takeielts.britishcouncil.org/take-ielts/prepare/free-ielts-english-practice-tests" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">British Council's familiarisation test page</a> is the closest thing to a free, full-length computer-delivered IELTS experience available without paying. It covers Listening, Reading, and Writing across a 2-hour 30-minute sitting, for both Academic and General Training. You can download the question papers and blank answer sheets, and you get free results for Listening and Reading. Writing isn't auto-scored, which reflects how the real test works. No advance registration is required.</p>
  <h3>3. IDP's free General Training and Academic resources</h3>
  <p>IDP, as a co-owner of the IELTS brand, provides free practice resources including General Training Listening tests with audio playback and practice materials. The resources align with official question types and are particularly useful for candidates sitting the General Training pathway for visa or registration purposes. Like the other official sources, the coverage skews toward Listening and Reading, with Writing and Speaking requiring you to self-assess. Note that the range of downloadable full-paper PDFs may be more limited here than through IELTS.org or the British Council.</p>
  <h2>3 reputable free platforms for timed online mock tests</h2>
  <p>Once you've completed the official materials, these third-party platforms add practice volume with browser-based timed mocks that approximate the computer-delivered test experience.</p>
  <h3>4. IELTS Online Tests: browser-based and continuously updated</h3>
  <p>IELTS Online Tests runs timed full-section mocks in a browser interface with scoring for Listening and Reading on selected free tests. The site states its materials are continuously updated to meet British Council and IDP standards. The free tier gives you access to a selection of full mocks, and the on-screen format mirrors how questions appear in the computer-delivered test. Writing and Speaking are not scored on the free plan, which is consistent with what most platforms offer at no cost.</p>
  <h3>5. Magoosh's free practice test and score report</h3>
  <p>Magoosh offers a free IELTS practice test that produces an estimated score by section and an overall total, along with strengths-and-weaknesses feedback. The platform covers all four skills across its content library, including free vocabulary flashcards, a podcast, and separate skill-based exercises. The free practice test is well-structured and useful for getting a baseline score, though the Writing and Speaking sections don't appear to provide criterion-level feedback on the free tier.</p>
  <h3>6. IELTSFreeTests and BestMyTest: full four-section simulations</h3>
  <p>IELTSFreeTests makes its first mock available for free with auto-scoring across sections, while BestMyTest self-reports a free 2.5-hour full simulation covering all four test areas. Both sites self-report their format accuracy, so treat them as high-volume practice tools rather than official benchmarks. Listening and Reading scores are useful for tracking progress; Writing and Speaking results on these platforms are either absent or too generic to be actionable on their own.</p>
  <p>This is the consistent gap across all three platforms: they score what can be auto-marked and leave the two most complex skills largely unevaluated. For candidates targeting a Band 7 or above, that's a significant blind spot.</p>
  <h2>Place #7: why IELTS Ai Pro's free mock tests go beyond a static PDF</h2>
  <p>Every source covered so far has the same fundamental limitation: Writing and Speaking either aren't included, aren't scored, or receive feedback so generic it doesn't tell you what to fix. That's the gap <a href="https://www.ieltsai.pro/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IELTS Ai Pro</a> is designed to address.</p>
  <h3>What the IELTS Ai Pro free tier actually includes</h3>
  <p>IELTS Ai Pro's free tier is designed to provide Cambridge-style full mock tests across all four skills: Reading, Listening, Writing, and Speaking. According to the platform, no credit card is required to access it. The free plan is positioned as genuinely functional rather than a teaser that locks every useful feature behind a paywall, a meaningful distinction for candidates who can't afford a prep course or weekly tutor sessions.</p>
  <h3>How AI scoring works across Writing and Speaking on a full mock</h3>
  <p>When you complete a Writing task, IELTS Ai Pro returns an AI-estimated band score broken down across the four official criteria: Task Achievement, Coherence and Cohesion, Lexical Resource, and Grammatical Range and Accuracy. Speaking responses are evaluated across Fluency, Vocabulary, Grammar, and Pronunciation. The feedback is criterion-referenced, not just a number. A PDF gives you the questions; IELTS Ai Pro gives you the questions, an estimated score, and specific feedback before you close the tab. That's a materially different study experience from a static download, and it's what makes full mock practice actually useful rather than just familiar.</p>
  <h2>Official vs. third-party: how to judge a free mock before you practise</h2>
  <p>Not every free IELTS test you find online is worth your time. Before you commit to a full 2.5-hour practice session, spend two minutes verifying the source, specifically, check whether the content is produced or licensed by a recognised IELTS organisation, and whether the audio files are original rather than re-recorded.</p>
  <h3>Red flags that indicate a low-quality free mock</h3>
  <p>The clearest warning signs are tests labelled "real leaked papers" or "official questions" without citing Cambridge, the British Council, or IDP as the source. No legitimate free mock uses actual past papers, because IELTS past papers are licenced and sold through Cambridge. Other red flags include PDFs without any audio component (a Listening test with no audio is not a Listening test), audio that sounds re-recorded rather than professionally produced, and materials that predate recent format changes to IELTS question conventions. If a site's answer key contains errors or disputes in the comments section, that's a strong signal the content wasn't produced by anyone familiar with IELTS marking standards.</p>
  <h3>What format accuracy actually means for your preparation</h3>
  <p>Format accuracy matters because practising with the wrong task types or incorrect timing creates false confidence. If your mock Reading section has 30 questions instead of 40, your timed performance means nothing. If your Writing Task 1 prompts are consistently different in style from official tasks, you're practising the wrong skill. Use official sources as your baseline and treat third-party mocks as supplementary volume, not as the primary reference for what the real test looks and feels like.</p>
  <h2>How to run a timed mock at home and actually improve from it</h2>
  <p>Finding a good free full-length IELTS practice exam is only half the equation. How you run it determines whether you improve.</p>
  <h3>Setting up conditions that match the real exam</h3>
  <p>Before you start: phone off, all other browser tabs closed, timer set to run without pauses. For Listening, wear headphones and play the audio once only, exactly as it runs in the test room. Complete Listening, Reading, and Writing back-to-back with no breaks between them, because that's the actual test structure. If you're practising a paper-based format, transfer your Listening answers to the answer sheet during the audio, not after, that skill is part of the test itself.</p>
  <h3>Turning your mock results into a focused improvement plan</h3>
  <p>Score your Listening and Reading immediately after completing the test, then log the result by section and by question type. The most common waste of study time is taking a full mock, checking the total score, feeling either relieved or discouraged, and moving on, that tells you almost nothing. Identify which question types caused the most errors, whether that's matching headings in Reading or note completion in Listening. Then revisit those specific types before sitting another full mock. For Writing and Speaking, platforms that offer criterion-level AI feedback can surface exactly where your score is dropping, so you're targeting specific weaknesses rather than guessing.</p>
  <h2>Put your first timed mock on the calendar this week</h2>
  <p>Here's the quick summary. Start with the official bodies for format accuracy: IELTS.org for downloadable PDFs with audio, British Council for the closest free computer-delivered simulation, and IDP for General Training practice materials. Add volume with IELTS Online Tests, Magoosh, and IELTSFreeTests for timed browser-based practice across Listening and Reading. Then use IELTS Ai Pro's free tier to address the Writing and Speaking gap that every other free option leaves open.</p>
  <p>Each category serves a different purpose: official sources give you the most accurate question formats; third-party sites give you practice volume with timed mocks; and a platform like IELTS Ai Pro adds the scoring and feedback layer that a static PDF simply cannot provide. You need all three working together.</p>
  <p>Pick one official source and one feedback-enabled platform, block two and a half hours in your calendar this week, and run your first timed full IELTS practice exam under real conditions. If you're still hunting for the right resource rather than actually sitting a complete mock, that search is costing you study time. Pick one and start.</p>
  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>Where can I find free full-length IELTS practice exams online?</h3>
  <p>The most reliable sources are IELTS.org (<a href="https://ielts.org/take-a-test/preparation-resources" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official downloadable PDFs with audio</a>), the British Council's familiarisation test page (free computer-delivered simulation), and IDP's free practice resources. For timed browser-based mocks, IELTS Online Tests, Magoosh, and IELTSFreeTests are reputable options. If you also need Writing and Speaking feedback, IELTS Ai Pro offers a free tier designed to provide AI-scored full mocks across all four skills.</p>
  <h3>Are free IELTS practice exams accurate enough to rely on?</h3>
  <p>Official sources (IELTS.org, British Council, IDP) are the most accurate because they're produced by the organisations that own the test. Reputable third-party platforms are useful for volume practice but should be treated as supplementary rather than definitive format references.</p>
  <h3>Do any free IELTS mock tests include Writing and Speaking scoring?</h3>
  <p>Most free platforms only auto-score Listening and Reading. IELTS Ai Pro is designed to provide criterion-referenced AI scoring for Writing and Speaking on its free tier, which is what sets it apart from static downloads and browser-based Listening/Reading mocks.</p>
  <p><strong>Related practice:</strong> <a href="/cambridge-ielts-practice-tests">Cambridge-style IELTS practice tests</a> and <a href="/blog/30-day-ielts-study-plan-band-7">30-day IELTS study plan</a>.</p>
