  <p>Looking for a Cambridge IELTS mock test free of charge, one that actually replicates the real exam? They exist, but finding legitimate options takes more than a quick search. IELTS test fees in the United States typically run well over $200, and paying another $30, $50, or more for practice materials on top of that is a genuine barrier, especially for self-study learners already stretching their budgets. The frustrating reality is that quality free options do exist, they just take some effort to separate from the noise. Many sites promise "official Cambridge tests" and deliver something poorly formatted, riddled with answer key errors, or simply a low-quality imitation of the real exam format.</p>
  <p>This guide cuts through that. You'll find out what actually makes a practice test Cambridge-style (and why that distinction matters for your prep), which official sources give you legitimate free materials, which third-party platforms offer the best full-length timed simulations, and how to turn your results into real score improvement rather than just a number on a screen.</p>
  <p>For learners who want the full picture in one place, Cambridge-style reading and listening mock tests plus instant AI feedback on writing and speaking, <a href="https://www.ieltsai.pro/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IELTS Ai Pro's free tier</a> is designed to cover more ground than most standalone mock test sites. That platform is covered in detail further down.</p>
  <h2>What "Cambridge-style" actually means for practice tests</h2>
  <p>The phrase gets thrown around loosely, so it's worth being precise. A genuinely Cambridge-style IELTS mock test has four defining markers: authentic question types, strict section timing, the correct number of questions per section, and a band score conversion table based on raw scores. Without all four, you're doing generic English practice, not exam prep.</p>
  <p>On question types, that means matching headings, multiple choice, and sentence completion for Reading; form-fill, diagram labeling, and short answer for Listening. The timing matters too: 60 minutes for Reading and 60 minutes for Writing. Listening runs approximately 30 minutes plus 10 minutes of transfer time on paper (built-in on computer), and Speaking runs 11, 14 minutes. The band score conversion is also non-linear: for Academic Reading, roughly 30, 32 correct answers out of 40 converts to Band 7, while General Training needs about 34, 35 correct for the same band. A practice test without these specifics cannot give you accurate diagnostic data.</p>
  <h3>Official distribution vs. third-party mirrors</h3>
  <p>The legitimate official sources are IELTS.org, <a href="https://www.cambridgeenglish.org/exams-and-tests/ielts/preparation/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cambridge English</a>, the British Council, and IDP, the co-owners and administrators of the exam. Any site hosting "Cambridge IELTS Books 1, 20 as free PDFs" is redistributing copyrighted material, not officially published content. This matters beyond the legal question: unofficial answer keys sometimes contain errors, and transcripts for listening sections are occasionally corrupted or incomplete. If your diagnostic data is wrong, your study plan will be wrong too.</p>
  <p>The <a href="https://www.ieltsai.pro/cambridge-ielts-practice-tests" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cambridge IELTS practice book series</a>, volumes 1 through 20, are all officially paid products. Cambridge does not release them as full free downloads. If a site claims otherwise, treat the content with skepticism and cross-check any answer keys you use.</p>
  <h2>Where to find a Cambridge IELTS mock test free: official sources</h2>
  <p>IELTS.org is your first stop for a cambridge ielts mock test free of charge from the actual test developers. The site provides free downloadable <a href="https://ielts.org/take-a-test/preparation-resources/sample-test-questions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">sample materials</a> for both Academic and General Training without requiring registration. For Academic, you get:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A Listening sample tasks PDF with a separate answer sheet and recording transcript</li>
  </ul>
  <ul>
    <li>An Academic Reading sample tasks PDF with its own answer sheet</li>
  </ul>
  <ul>
    <li>An Academic Writing sample tasks PDF</li>
  </ul>
  <ul>
    <li>A Listening Multiple Choice sample with a separate answer key</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These materials are guaranteed to match the real exam format because they come directly from the test developers.</p>
  <p>Cambridge English provides one free sample paper download and links back to the official IELTS sample question sets. Registration is required to access the Cambridge English practice resources, but the free tier is genuinely useful. The British Council's <a href="https://takeielts.britishcouncil.org/take-ielts/prepare/free-ielts-english-practice-tests/ielts-on-computer/how-it-works" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TakeIELTS site</a> also provides free practice materials for both Academic and General Training, including timed practice sections and model answers with examiner commentary. These official sources are the safest starting point for understanding the real exam structure before moving to full-length simulations.</p>
  <p>Be clear about what these official sources do not give you: the complete Cambridge IELTS book series. Those are paid products. The one exception worth noting is that Cambridge has occasionally released individual volumes as promotional digital offers. Cambridge IELTS 19 General Training appeared as a free digital item on the Cambridge shop at one point, but this is the exception, not the rule.</p>
  <h2>Best third-party platforms for full-length timed simulations</h2>
  <p>Once you've confirmed you understand the format from official materials, full-length timed practice under realistic conditions is the logical next step. Several third-party platforms deliver this without charging for access.</p>
  <p>Langogh stands out because it offers a full-length Academic mock test in the browser with no PDF format and no sign-up required. On-screen timers match official IELTS limits, and the experience is closer to the computer-delivered exam than a printable document ever can be. LandingPrep provides a complete four-skill timed mock with instant auto-scoring and a full review screen after submission. IELTSMate and IELTS Test Pro both offer timed, full-length simulations with authentic section ordering and question types. IELTS Online Tests maintains a large free library of practice tests available without registration.</p>
  <p>The computer-delivered format is now widely available at U.S. test centers, and the interface differs from paper in ways worth experiencing before exam day. On screen, you can highlight text, navigate freely between questions, and type your writing directly into text fields with a live word count. A browser-based simulator exposes you to those mechanics in a way that a printed PDF simply cannot.</p>
  <h3>IELTS Ai Pro's free reading and listening tests</h3>
  <p>IELTS Ai Pro's free tier is designed to include Cambridge-style timed reading and listening mock tests with saved attempt history, which means you can track performance across multiple sessions rather than just checking answers in isolation. The format follows the same question types and timing as the official exam. What separates this from a single-use test generator is that your reading and listening results are intended to feed into an adaptive study plan built on your full practice history, so rather than scoring a test and wondering what to do next, the platform points you in a direction. Check the IELTS Ai Pro feature documentation for the current scope of the free tier.</p>
  <h2>The feedback gap: writing and speaking need more than an answer key</h2>
  <p>Reading and listening are straightforward to self-score. You compare your answers to the key, count the correct ones, and look up the band score on the conversion table. Writing and speaking are a different problem entirely. IELTS Writing is scored across four official criteria simultaneously: Task Achievement, Coherence and Cohesion, Lexical Resource, and Grammatical Range and Accuracy. A Band 9 model essay tells you what a top response looks like, but it doesn't tell you why your essay scored lower or which of the four criteria is pulling your band down the most.</p>
  <p>This is the gap that makes free mock tests incomplete on their own. Most platforms provide answer keys for reading and listening, some provide model essays, and a few include examiner commentary. None of that feedback, however, is specific to your response. Reading a Band 9 sample next to your own essay and guessing at the difference is not the same as receiving criterion-referenced feedback on what you actually wrote.</p>
  <h3>How AI feedback closes the loop</h3>
  <p>After finishing a full-length mock on any of the platforms above, you can submit your writing tasks to IELTS Ai Pro for AI feedback tied directly to the four official IELTS criteria. According to the platform, the evaluation includes inline corrections and specific next-step recommendations, not just a score. The speaking evaluator is designed to score recorded responses across Fluency, Vocabulary, Grammar, and Pronunciation, the same four criteria a real examiner applies. A static answer key simply cannot address these dimensions. The combination of a free mock test from a reputable simulator and AI-powered feedback from IELTS Ai Pro covers the full diagnostic loop that any single platform working alone cannot replicate.</p>
  <h2>How to run a real diagnostic session and act on the results</h2>
  <p>A mock test only functions as a diagnostic tool if you run it under real conditions. That means no pausing, no dictionary lookups, no phone, and completing all four sections in a single sitting, roughly 2 hours and 45 minutes for Academic IELTS. Use a browser-based timed platform rather than a PDF to match the computer-delivered format. Treat the environment seriously, because exam hall conditions reveal stamina and concentration issues that relaxed practice at a coffee shop will not expose.</p>
  <p>After you finish, score reading and listening against the answer key and look up your raw score on the band conversion table. For Academic Reading, use the Academic scale, not the General Training scale; the cutoffs are different, and using the wrong one skews your self-assessment. Then route your writing and speaking responses through an AI evaluation tool for criterion-referenced feedback before you do anything else.</p>
  <p>The most useful thing to look for in your results is patterns, not isolated errors. A consistent problem with matching headings in Reading is a strategy issue, not a vocabulary gap. Repeated low scores on Coherence and Cohesion in Writing point to paragraph structure and logical flow, not grammar drills. Knowing which category of problem you're dealing with lets you prioritize the next two to three weeks of practice in a targeted way. Running another full mock test immediately is rarely the best next move; acting on the data from the one you just completed usually is.</p>
  <h2>Putting it all together</h2>
  <p>The most effective approach to free IELTS preparation is layered. Begin with official sample materials from IELTS.org or the British Council to confirm you understand the real format and question types. From there, shift to a reputable browser-based simulator for full-length practice under timed exam conditions. Finally, close the feedback gap for writing and speaking with AI-powered evaluation. No single free resource delivers all three of those things reliably, combining them does.</p>
  <p>Cambridge IELTS mock test free options are genuinely available if you know where to look and which sources to trust. The real investment isn't money, it's knowing how to use the results. Treating each mock test as a diagnostic session and routing writing and speaking into a tool like IELTS Ai Pro supports faster, more deliberate progress than simply collecting test scores without acting on them. Run the test properly, score it against the right table, get criterion-referenced feedback, and let those results drive your next study block.</p>
  <p><strong>Related practice:</strong> <a href="/cambridge-ielts-practice-tests">Cambridge-style IELTS practice tests</a> and <a href="/blog/ielts-mock-test-score-readiness-checklist">mock-test readiness checklist</a>.</p>
