  <p>If you've ever searched for the best free IELTS practice test online, you already know the problem: dozens of sites claim to be the top option, but most of them auto-score Reading and Listening, hand you a band number, and offer only limited or automated feedback for Writing and Speaking. You spend an hour on a full mock test, get a 6.5 with no explanation, and have no idea whether that number reflects your actual level or a shaky conversion table.</p>
  <p>That's the core problem with free IELTS prep in 2026: there are plenty of mock tests online, but very few genuine preparation tools. The specific capabilities that are scarce across free platforms include criterion-level writing feedback, multi-session performance tracking, and adaptive study guidance. This guide evaluates the strongest free options across four dimensions that actually matter: test authenticity, scoring accuracy, feedback quality, and user experience. It also covers how to combine these resources into a study plan that moves your band score rather than just measuring it.</p>
  <h2>What actually makes a free IELTS practice test worth your time</h2>
  <p>Before comparing platforms, you need a framework for judging them. Not every free mock test is built the same, and the differences between them directly affect how well your preparation transfers to the real exam.</p>
  <h3>Question type authenticity and test format</h3>
  <p>The real IELTS exam uses specific question formats: multiple choice, sentence completion, matching headings, short answer, and several others depending on the section. Platforms that substitute simplified question types train habits that don't apply on test day. If a free Reading test uses only multiple choice questions when the real exam mixes five or six formats across three passages, you're practicing for the wrong test. Strict timing per section also matters. A tool without a proper section timer gives you a false sense of readiness, because IELTS is as much a time management test as a language test. British Council and IDP set the format standard, and any third-party tool worth using mirrors their structure closely.</p>
  <h3>Scoring accuracy: where most free tools fall short</h3>
  <p>Listening and Reading can be objectively scored using an answer key and the <a href="https://ielts.org/take-a-test/your-results/ielts-scoring-in-detail" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official band conversion table</a>, which maps correct answers out of 40 to bands on the 9-point scale. Most reputable platforms handle this reasonably well because the math is straightforward.</p>
  <p>Writing and Speaking are a different problem entirely. The real IELTS exam uses trained human examiners who assess four specific criteria for Writing (Task Achievement, Coherence and Cohesion, Lexical Resource, and Grammatical Range and Accuracy) and four for Speaking (Fluency, Vocabulary, Grammar, and Pronunciation). Any platform producing a Writing band score without criterion-level analysis is giving you a number, not an assessment. Studies on AI scoring accuracy indicate that automated tools can differ from human raters by around 0.4 band points on average and often score higher, which means uncritical reliance on AI scores can create a false ceiling on your perceived progress.</p>
  <h3>Feedback depth: the gap between a score and an improvement</h3>
  <p>A score without feedback is just a number. The most useful free tools provide either model answers with examiner commentary or AI-generated criterion-level feedback that tells you exactly where your response fell short and what to fix. Platforms that skip this step force you to repeat the same mistakes across every attempt. One full mock test reviewed carefully is worth more than three taken back-to-back with no analysis in between.</p>
  <h2>What is the best free IELTS practice test online? Start with the official sources</h2>
  <p>The official sources are the right starting point for anyone who hasn't yet taken a full-length practice test under timed conditions.</p>
  <h3>What the official platforms actually offer for free</h3>
  <p>The British Council's <a href="https://takeielts.britishcouncil.org/take-ielts/prepare/free-ielts-english-practice-tests" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IELTS Ready</a> free tier includes a 50-minute Mini Mock Test with AI feedback and an estimated band score, plus two full mock tests with feedback and model answers. If you book your test directly through the British Council, IELTS Ready Premium unlocks 40 full practice tests at no additional cost, a significant advantage. The British Council also provides a Familiarisation Test specifically designed for IELTS Online, which replicates the interface and format of the computer-delivered exam. IELTS.org offers sample test papers and practice questions that accurately represent the official format. These remain the most reliable representations of the real exam available anywhere for free.</p>
  <h3>The gap official resources leave open</h3>
  <p>Official free tools are strong on format accuracy and weak on personalized feedback. IELTS.org sample papers generally do not include AI band estimates or multi-session performance tracking, though some British Council offerings, such as IELTS Ready, do provide AI feedback and estimated band scores for certain test types. What the official suite doesn't offer, even at its best, is adaptive guidance that adjusts to your history across sessions. Official resources are excellent for understanding the test structure but not for diagnosing why your score is stuck at 6.0 instead of 7.0. For that kind of targeted analysis, you need something beyond what official sources provide.</p>
  <h2>Best free IELTS practice test online: third-party platforms worth trying</h2>
  <p>Third-party platforms fill the volume and variety gap that official sources leave open. They vary widely in quality, but several stand out for specific use cases.</p>
  <h3>Platforms worth trying</h3>
  <p>IELTS Online Tests, IELTSFreeTests.com, Langogh, LinguaPractice, and UniCoachify all offer genuinely full-length, timed mock exams across Listening and Reading with auto-scoring. Langogh stands out for one practical reason: it requires no sign-up for the free test, which removes the friction that causes many learners to abandon a site before finishing a single session. LinguaPractice covers both Academic and General Training formats, which matters if you're preparing for General Training for immigration or work-based visa purposes. IELTS Mock Lab provides Task 1 and Task 2 writing prompts with band 9 model answers and expert analysis at no cost, making it a strong supplementary resource for Writing practice specifically. If you prefer a free IELTS practice app over a browser-based tool, several of these platforms have mobile-optimized versions worth exploring alongside their web interfaces.</p>
  <h3>How reliable are their band score estimates?</h3>
  <p>For Listening and Reading, most of these platforms produce reliable estimates because they use the same raw-score-to-band conversion logic as the official exam. Correct answers are counted out of 40 and mapped to a band using a lookup table. Several platforms explicitly state they use official conversion tables, IELTS Online Tests, for example, publishes this methodology openly.</p>
  <p>Writing and Speaking are less consistent. Some platforms warn that their Speaking scores should not be treated as equivalent to official IELTS results, which is the honest approach. Others are less transparent. Before relying on a Writing score from any free platform, verify that the feedback references all four official Writing criteria with criterion-level scores rather than producing a generic grammar summary with a band attached.</p>
  <h3>What's still missing from the free tier on most platforms</h3>
  <p>Most third-party tools stop at a score. They don't explain why your Reading band dropped between attempts, which question types you consistently miss, or what to practice next. That gap, between a testing tool and a preparation tool, is where measurable differences in study outcomes appear.</p>
  <h2>IELTS Ai Pro: when free practice meets real AI feedback</h2>
  <p>Unlike most free platforms that deliver a score and little else, IELTS Ai Pro is designed to give you a direction. According to the platform, its free entry tier includes <a href="https://www.ieltsai.pro/cambridge-ielts-practice-tests" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cambridge-style mock tests</a> for Reading and Listening with timed conditions, AI Writing feedback with band score estimates aligned to all four official IELTS Writing criteria (Task Achievement, Coherence and Cohesion, Lexical Resource, and Grammatical Range and Accuracy), and AI Speaking practice scored across Fluency, Vocabulary, Grammar, and Pronunciation. Saved attempt history is also part of the free tier, letting you track score trends across sessions rather than treating each attempt in isolation.</p>
  <h3>How the AI scoring compares to what other free tools provide</h3>
  <p>Most free mock test platforms auto-score Reading and Listening competently but produce Writing and Speaking scores through vague AI analysis or skip them entirely. IELTS Ai Pro claims its Writing feedback includes inline corrections, criterion-level band scores, and next-step recommendations tied to the specific submission, a meaningful step up from the generic grammar summaries most free tools attach to a band number. The practical distinction is that other tools tell you your score; IELTS Ai Pro aims to tell you why you got that score and what to address before the next attempt.</p>
  <h3>The adaptive study path: what changes when the platform knows your history</h3>
  <p>After each practice session, IELTS Ai Pro's Adaptive Learning Path is said to recommend focused next activities based on completed practice, skill performance, target band score, and study schedule. For self-study learners without a tutor, that kind of structure replaces the guesswork of deciding what to practice next. Instead of taking random mock tests and hoping the score improves, you get direction tied to actual performance data, turning practice sessions into a purposeful preparation sequence rather than a series of disconnected attempts.</p>
  <h2>Writing and Speaking feedback on free platforms: what you're really getting</h2>
  <p>The hardest part of free IELTS prep is getting honest, actionable feedback on productive skills without paying for a tutor. Here's how the free landscape actually breaks down.</p>
  <h3>AI feedback vs. human expert feedback: the honest comparison</h3>
  <p>Most free platforms now advertise AI feedback, but quality varies significantly. Lingozy is one of the few platforms that offers human teacher feedback for free Writing submissions, a genuine differentiator in a market where most feedback is automated. For AI tools, the key question is whether the feedback evaluates your response against the four official IELTS Writing criteria with criterion-level scores, or just runs a grammar check and attaches a band number. The former is genuinely useful for preparation. The latter tells you very little about why your Task Achievement score is a 5 when you need a 7.</p>
  <h3>Model answers and examiner commentary: which free tools include them</h3>
  <p><a href="https://ieltsbeacon.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IELTS Beacon</a> and LinguaPractice both offer model answers for Writing Task 1, Task 2, and Speaking with examiner-style commentary explaining what earned the high band score. <a href="https://ieltsliz.com/ielts-writing-task-1-lessons-and-tips/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IELTS Liz</a> provides free model answers and lessons for Writing and Speaking as well. The right way to use these resources is not to memorize model responses but to reverse-engineer the structure and vocabulary choices that produced a high band score. Combine that habit with criterion-based AI feedback and you have a self-study loop that actively closes skill gaps.</p>
  <h2>How to build a study plan around free mock tests</h2>
  <p>Access to good resources matters less than knowing how to sequence them, and how much time you invest in analysis between attempts.</p>
  <h3>Sequencing your mock test practice</h3>
  <p>Start with an official British Council or IELTS.org familiarisation test to understand the format before anything else. Once you know the structure, move to full-length mocks on a third-party platform for volume. One full mock test per week works well during the early preparation phase. Expert guidance generally recommends maintaining that pace through the final month, with the option to increase to two per week in the last two to three weeks if stamina or time management is a specific concern. Reviewing a mock test carefully can take as long as the test itself, depending on how deeply you analyze wrong answers and criterion scores. If you're not spending meaningful time on analysis, you're leaving most of the learning on the table.</p>
  <h3>Using feedback to close skill gaps between attempts</h3>
  <p>Treat every mock test score as a diagnostic, not a verdict. For Reading and Listening, identify which question types drove the incorrect answers, not just how many you got wrong overall. For Writing and Speaking, look at criterion scores to find the weakest area. If your Coherence and Cohesion score is a full band below your Task Achievement, your problem is paragraph structure and linking, not content. That specific finding should drive the following week's focused practice.</p>
  <p>IELTS Ai Pro's Progress Analytics dashboard is designed to make this process systematic, with score trends across all four skills visible in one place. Whether you use that tool or maintain your own tracking spreadsheet, the principle is the same: patterns only emerge across multiple attempts, and a single mock test score is never enough data to plan from.</p>
  <h2>Choosing the right free tool for where you are right now</h2>
  <p>So what is the best free IELTS practice test online? There's no single answer, it depends on your preparation stage and which skills need the most attention.</p>
  <p>For format accuracy and official test experience, British Council and IELTS.org are the right starting point. For volume and variety across full-length mocks, IELTS Online Tests, LinguaPractice, and IELTSFreeTests.com fill the gap effectively. For model answers with examiner-style commentary, IELTS Beacon and IELTS Liz add genuine value for Writing and Speaking analysis. For criterion-level AI feedback that connects your practice to a structured improvement path, <a href="https://www.ieltsai.pro/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IELTS Ai Pro's free tier</a> offers a more complete feedback loop than most free alternatives, particularly for learners who need Writing and Speaking guidance without the cost of a private tutor.</p>
  <p>Start with a familiarisation test, add regular full-length mocks to build stamina, and use criterion-based AI feedback to make each session purposeful. That combination covers what no single platform delivers alone and gives you a systematic path toward your target band score, rather than a series of numbers with no clear next step attached.</p>
