  <p>Most students applying to US universities from Australia spend weeks trying to answer the wrong question. They scroll through Reddit threads, work through forum posts, and end up more confused than when they started. Here is the honest answer: both IELTS Academic and TOEFL iBT are accepted at almost all US universities, and there is no universal admissions advantage to picking one over the other. The real question, which is easier, IELTS or TOEFL for US university admission, comes down to which test format plays to your individual strengths, because that is where your score goes up.</p>
  <p>If you are leaning toward IELTS, one practical way to benchmark yourself before registering is to run a free AI-powered mock test on <strong>IELTS Ai Pro</strong>. You will get an estimated band score across all four skills, giving you real data to weigh against your target university's minimum requirements. That takes about two hours and costs nothing. Paying a registration fee first and discovering your weak spots afterward is a worse plan.</p>
  <h2>Do US universities actually treat these tests equally?</h2>
  <p>The short answer is yes. Almost every US university accepts both <a href="https://ielts.org/take-a-test/test-types/ielts-academic-test" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IELTS Academic</a> and TOEFL iBT for undergraduate and graduate admission. There is no formal institutional preference built into most admissions processes. If you meet the required score, either test works.</p>
  <p>The caveat worth knowing: some individual programmes or departments have format-specific rules around score reporting, so it is always worth checking the admissions page for your specific school and programme before you register. This matters more at the graduate level, where programme requirements can differ from the university's general policy.</p>
  <p>TOEFL does carry a familiarity edge at highly selective US graduate programmes. Schools like Harvard, MIT, Stanford, and Yale formally accept IELTS, but their admissions guidance has historically given more prominence to TOEFL, a legacy of TOEFL being the default US-based English proficiency test for decades. This is not a formal preference, and it should not push you toward TOEFL if IELTS genuinely suits you better. If you are targeting competitive postgraduate programmes at elite schools, that context is worth factoring in, but check each programme's current admissions page directly to confirm requirements.</p>
  <h2>Which is easier for US university admission: IELTS or TOEFL by structure?</h2>
  <p>TOEFL iBT runs for approximately two hours total, including a ten-minute break. IELTS Academic runs for two hours and 45 minutes, with no scheduled break during the Listening, Reading, and Writing sections. Speaking is conducted as a separate live interview, sometimes on a different day. The total time difference is not dramatic, but the two experiences feel very different.</p>
  <p>For reading, IELTS gives you three passages across 60 minutes with 40 questions and a wide variety of question types: matching headings, short answer, True/False/Not Given, diagram labelling, and more. TOEFL iBT typically gives you two passages in around 35 minutes with 20 questions, and the question style is more uniform. IELTS gives you more time overall, but the variety of formats demands more adaptability.</p>
  <p>For listening, IELTS uses four fixed recordings totalling 30 minutes of audio, with a ten-minute paper transfer buffer on the paper-based version. TOEFL listening is fully computer-mediated and integrates with the overall test flow.</p>
  <p>Speaking is where the two tests diverge most sharply. TOEFL speaking is recorded into a computer: you respond to prompts and your audio is scored later by ETS using its established scoring procedures. IELTS speaking is a live, face-to-face interview with a certified examiner conducted across three parts over 11 to 14 minutes. One rewards composure under machine conditions; the other rewards natural conversational ability, and for many candidates, this single factor is what determines which test they should sit.</p>
  <p>For writing, IELTS Academic uses a two-task format: a 150-word data description (Task 1) followed by a 250-word discursive essay (Task 2), totalling 60 minutes. TOEFL iBT's current writing section uses shorter, more integrated tasks including an academic discussion response, running approximately 29 to 50 minutes. Many candidates report that IELTS Task 2 feels more familiar if their academic background involves traditional essay writing. TOEFL's integrated tasks reward reading-response skills more than standalone essay structure.</p>
  <h2>Which sections tend to trip people up on each test</h2>
  <p>Survey data and online forums consistently flag IELTS Reading and Writing as the two sections candidates find hardest. Reading in particular catches people out because of the question type diversity: True/False/Not Given and matching headings generate consistent complaints. The 60-minute window sounds generous, but the range of formats leaves little margin for re-reading, and running out of time on the third passage is common.</p>
  <p>For speaking and writing, the right test depends on your honest self-assessment. Candidates who are comfortable in spontaneous conversation typically prefer IELTS's live interview format. Candidates who find it easier to organise their thoughts without someone watching them often perform better with TOEFL's recorded format. Neither is inherently easier. They are structurally different, and your performance on each will depend on which environment suits how you actually communicate.</p>
  <h2>Translating IELTS band scores to TOEFL scores</h2>
  <p>The <a href="https://www.ets.org/Media/Research/pdf/RR-25-02.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official ETS and IELTS equivalency chart</a> is the standard reference used by US universities when comparing scores across the two tests. The range that matters most for US admission runs from IELTS 6.0 to 8.0.</p>
  <p>IELTS Academic band TOEFL iBT equivalent (0, 120 scale)</p>
  <p>8.0110, 114 7.5102, 109 7.094, 101 6.579, 93 6.060, 78</p>
  <p>In practice, many top-50 US universities typically ask for IELTS 6.5 to 7.0 for undergraduate admission and 7.0 to 7.5 for graduate entry. Schools like MIT, Stanford, Harvard, and Yale cluster around IELTS 7.0, while programmes at Northwestern, Carnegie Mellon, and NYU tend to sit at 7.5. If a school's admissions page lists TOEFL 100 on the old 0, 120 scale as its benchmark, that falls within the IELTS 7.0, 7.5 range, more precisely, near the top of the IELTS 7.0 band. When in doubt, contact the admissions office directly to confirm the exact equivalent they will accept.</p>
  <p>One critical update for 2026: TOEFL iBT results from January 2026 onward use a new 1.0 to 6.0 scoring scale, and these scores cannot be directly compared with pre-2026 TOEFL iBT results or with the traditional IELTS equivalency chart above. If a US university's admissions page still lists TOEFL requirements based on the old 0, 120 scale, contact the admissions office directly to confirm current requirements before submitting scores. <a href="https://www.ets.org/toefl/china/toefl/score-scale-update.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Check the ETS website</a> for the official announcement and updated guidance. This is not a minor footnote, acting on outdated score thresholds is an avoidable mistake.</p>
  <h2>Cost and scheduling practicalities in Australia</h2>
  <p>IELTS fees in Australia currently range from approximately AUD 395 to AUD 475 depending on the testing centre and city. Computer-delivered IELTS is available almost every day at participating centres, with results returned within three to five business days. <a href="https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/ielts-test-changes-2026-computer-writing-on-paper" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Paper-based IELTS</a> runs once or twice a month per centre, with results in approximately 13 days. Centres operate across major cities including Sydney, Melbourne, Brisbane, Perth, Adelaide, and Canberra, as well as a number of regional locations, making IELTS genuinely accessible for candidates outside the capital cities.</p>
  <p>TOEFL iBT is available at approved test centres in Australian capital cities on a fully computer-based format. Current Australian pricing from ETS sits at approximately USD 288, which translates to around AUD 415 to 475 depending on the exchange rate and applicable taxes. Scheduling availability in major Australian cities is often similar to <a href="https://ielts.com.au/australia/test-dates" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">computer-delivered IELTS</a>, but check directly with ETS or Pearson VUE for confirmed dates and current fees before booking, as pricing can shift with exchange rates.</p>
  <h2>Which test is easier for US university admission based on your profile?</h2>
  <p>TOEFL is the stronger choice if you are targeting competitive US graduate programmes where TOEFL carries historical familiarity, if you are more comfortable with fully computer-based tasks, or if you find recorded speaking less stressful than a live examiner interview. It also suits candidates who find integrated reading-and-writing tasks more natural than constructing standalone academic essays.</p>
  <p>IELTS is the better fit if you are more confident in face-to-face conversation than in speaking to a microphone, if your academic writing background aligns well with the Task 2 essay format, or if your target school lists IELTS minimums clearly and your preparation resources are already built around IELTS criteria. Before you register, run a mock test through <strong>IELTS Ai Pro</strong>'s free AI scoring tool to get a baseline band score estimate across all four skills. It shows you exactly where you stand before you spend money on a registration fee, so your preparation targets a real gap rather than a guess.</p>
  <h2>Stop comparing and start measuring</h2>
  <p>Both tests are widely accepted, and neither is universally easier. The right choice comes down to which format gives you the best shot at hitting the band score your target programme requires, and the only reliable way to know that is to practise under actual test conditions.</p>
  <p>If IELTS is the direction you are leaning, the next step is straightforward: take a <a href="https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/ielts-mock-test-score-readiness-checklist" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">timed mock test</a>, get your AI-estimated band score from <strong>IELTS Ai Pro</strong>, and compare it against your target school's minimum. You will have real data to make this decision within a couple of hours, rather than spending another week reading forum threads. That is a better use of your prep time.</p>
