  <p>If you're asking yourself, "Should I take IELTS or TOEFL if applying to US universities from Australia?", you're not alone. You've received a US university offer, congratulations, and now you're staring at the English proficiency requirement on the admissions page, wondering which test to book while the clock is already running. It's a surprisingly common point of paralysis for Australian applicants, given that most have been speaking English their entire lives and the test can feel like a formality, until you realise the wrong choice costs you weeks of misdirected preparation.</p>
  <p>Both IELTS and TOEFL are accepted at the vast majority of US institutions, so neither choice will close doors. But the decision still matters. The two tests differ in format, scoring logic, preparation approach, and practical logistics across Australia. For most Australians, those differences tend to point in one direction. This guide walks you through acceptance rates, format comparisons, score equivalence, Australian test costs, and why IELTS is often perceived as the stronger fit for local applicants.</p>
  <p>Australian applicants are increasingly using AI-powered platforms to prepare for IELTS specifically because the structured, criterion-based feedback removes the guesswork that makes test preparation so frustrating. We'll come back to that once we've covered the core comparison.</p>
  <h2>Should I take IELTS or TOEFL? Acceptance and requirements at US universities</h2>
  <p>Over 3,400 US institutions accept IELTS, and TOEFL is similarly accepted across most major US universities. In practice, the overwhelming majority of schools list both tests equally in their admissions requirements. If you're worried about one being more legitimate than the other in American eyes, you can set that concern aside.</p>
  <p>TOEFL has a longer history in the US admissions system, which means some departments or graduate programmes may list it first or more prominently in their materials. That is a presentation habit, not a signal that TOEFL scores carry more weight in the evaluation process. Ivy League schools and major research universities, including Stanford, Columbia, Yale, NYU, and Carnegie Mellon, all accept IELTS. The same is true for most public research universities and mid-tier institutions.</p>
  <p>The one practical check you must always do: look at the specific programme page, not just the university homepage. Acceptance can vary between undergraduate and postgraduate levels, and occasionally between departments within the same institution. A university's main admissions page might list both tests, while a particular graduate school of engineering lists only TOEFL. That programme-level verification takes five minutes and prevents a nasty surprise later.</p>
  <p>If a programme genuinely lists only one test, follow that instruction. If both are listed, choose the test where your preparation history, learning style, and timeline give you the best shot at reaching the required score. It's a practical decision, not a prestige one. The Duolingo English Test and PTE Academic are also gaining acceptance at many US universities, which we'll cover later in this guide.</p>
  <h2>Test format side-by-side: IELTS Academic vs TOEFL iBT</h2>
  <h3>Structure, timing, and delivery</h3>
  <p>IELTS Academic comprises four separately timed sections: Listening (approximately 30 minutes), Reading (60 minutes), Writing (60 minutes), and Speaking (11 to 14 minutes). Total test time sits at roughly two hours and 45 minutes. The Speaking section is conducted with a human examiner, either in person or via video link. <a href="https://www.usnews.com/education/best-colleges/articles/the-complete-guide-to-the-toefl-test" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TOEFL iBT delivers all four skills</a> in a single computer session of approximately 90 minutes in the current format. There is no human examiner; your speaking responses are recorded and assessed by a combination of AI and human raters.</p>
  <p>The question formats differ substantially. IELTS uses a wide variety of item types across Listening and Reading: multiple choice, matching, short answer, sentence completion, and diagram labelling, among others. TOEFL relies more heavily on integrated tasks, where you read or listen to source material and then respond in speaking or writing. For Australian students who have spent years in a British-style education system, the IELTS question formats tend to feel more intuitive, particularly in Reading and Writing, where the academic conventions and vocabulary expectations align more naturally with local schooling.</p>
  <p>IELTS now offers computer-delivered testing at most Australian centres alongside paper-based options. TOEFL iBT is computer-delivered only. Computer-delivered IELTS results are typically returned within three to five days; paper-based IELTS takes up to 13 days. TOEFL iBT result delivery times vary, check the ETS website for current timing. If you're working to a tight application deadline, result turnaround is worth factoring into your booking decision.</p>
  <h2>Score equivalence and what US programmes actually require</h2>
  <p><a href="https://www.ets.org/toefl/institutions/ibt/score-scale-update.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The ETS official equivalence table</a> is the most widely cited IELTS band to TOEFL score conversion reference in US admissions. The key benchmarks: IELTS 6.5 is broadly equivalent to TOEFL iBT 79 to 93; IELTS 7.0 sits at approximately TOEFL iBT 94 to 101; IELTS 7.5 maps to roughly TOEFL iBT 102 to 109. These figures provide a useful working guide, but universities set their own minimums and do not always translate scores mechanically from one test to the other.</p>
  <p>In terms of what programmes actually ask for: most mid-tier US universities require IELTS 6.5 or a TOEFL iBT score in the 79 to 90 range. More selective programmes and Ivy League institutions typically require IELTS 7.0 to 7.5, or TOEFL iBT 94 to 109. Postgraduate programmes in health, law, and engineering often sit at the higher end of these thresholds. Always verify the precise requirement at programme level.</p>
  <p>The most useful thing you can do before you start preparing is set a personal target score. Check the three universities you're most likely to apply to, take the highest minimum score from that list, then add 0.5 of a band (or five TOEFL points) as a buffer. That becomes your preparation target. Aiming at the minimum is a strategy for retakes; aiming slightly above builds confidence on test day.</p>
  <h2>Cost, test dates, and logistics across Australia</h2>
  <h3>IELTS Academic pricing and availability</h3>
  <p>IELTS Academic costs AUD 490 in Australia. Computer-delivered sessions are available almost every day at centres in Sydney, Melbourne, Brisbane, Perth, Adelaide, Canberra, Gold Coast, Hobart, Darwin, and a range of regional cities including Wollongong, Townsville, Cairns, Geelong, and Fremantle, among others. That frequency is a genuine logistical advantage: if you receive your results and want to resit before an application deadline, you can typically find a seat within days rather than weeks.</p>
  <h3>TOEFL iBT pricing and availability</h3>
  <p><a href="https://oneaustraliagroup.com/toefl-test-price" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TOEFL iBT is priced in USD</a>, currently around USD 288 (approximately AUD 445 at mid-2026 exchange rates; verify the current figure on the ETS Australia page before booking). Test centres are available in major Australian cities, but with noticeably fewer available dates and seats than IELTS computer-delivered sessions. Reduced scheduling flexibility is a real trade-off to weigh against any price difference. If you need a retake before a deadline, fewer available dates creates pressure you don't need.</p>
  <p>Both tests allow rescheduling, subject to a rescheduling fee. The practical advice here is to map your application deadlines first, then work backwards. Allow time for at least one retake window between your planned test date and the admissions deadline. Building that buffer in is simply good planning, not pessimism.</p>
  <h2>Why IELTS tends to suit Australian applicants better</h2>
  <h3>Choosing between IELTS and TOEFL for US university applications from Australia</h3>
  <p>Australian students are educated in a British English environment. Spelling conventions, grammar instruction, and academic writing norms all align more closely with IELTS, which is jointly developed and administered by Cambridge Assessment English, the British Council, and IDP. TOEFL iBT uses North American English conventions throughout, including in the Reading passages and Writing prompts. That difference is felt most sharply in the Writing section, where IELTS Academic Task 2 expects an essay style that will feel familiar to anyone who has written academic work in an Australian school or university.</p>
  <p>The Speaking format is also worth considering. A face-to-face conversation with a trained examiner, even via video link, suits many Australian learners better than recording spoken responses into a microphone for automated scoring. Research suggests that preferences vary between individuals: some find the human interaction more natural, while others prefer the lower-stakes feel of a recorded response. For applicants who have practised in conversational settings, the IELTS format tends to be a comfortable fit.</p>
  <p>One of the biggest anxiety points in IELTS preparation is not knowing where your Writing or Speaking band score actually sits until test day. This is where <strong><a href="https://www.ieltsai.pro/about" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IELTS Ai Pro</a></strong> can make a difference in your preparation. The platform provides <a href="https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/ai-ielts-speaking-score-accuracy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AI-estimated band scores</a> across all four skills with feedback mapped to IELTS scoring criteria, giving you a clearer picture of where you stand before the actual test. An adaptive study plan built from your practice history then directs each session toward the specific skills holding your score back.</p>
  <p>Split this into two focused stages. First, use your early practice results to identify the gap between your current score and your target. Then use that data to structure your sessions, rather than repeating ground you've already covered.</p>
  <p>For Australians starting from a strong English base, the typical structured preparation timeline to reach IELTS 7.0 is six to ten weeks. A practical milestone structure looks like this:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Weeks 1 to 2: run a diagnostic, identify which skills sit below your target band</li>
    <li>Weeks 3 to 6: targeted section-by-section practice with criterion-based feedback</li>
    <li>Weeks 7 to 8: full timed mock tests under exam conditions</li>
    <li>Weeks 9 to 10: score review, final polish, test booking confirmed</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Starting earlier reduces pressure and gives you a genuine retake window if the first sitting doesn't land where you need it.</p>
  <h2>Alternatives when IELTS and TOEFL aren't the right fit</h2>
  <p>PTE Academic is accepted at a wide range of US universities and processes results in as little as 48 hours. It is worth considering if you've consistently found the IELTS or TOEFL Speaking section difficult, as PTE Speaking is entirely AI-scored. Some test-takers find the format less pressured without an examiner present. As a broad equivalence guide, PTE 58 to 64 is comparable to IELTS 6.5 to 7.0, but verify the specific minimum with your target programme before treating it as a direct swap.</p>
  <p>The Duolingo English Test is accepted by a growing number of US institutions, including several Ivy League and major research universities. It is delivered entirely online, costs significantly less than either IELTS or TOEFL, and returns results within 48 hours. The critical caveat is that acceptance is not universal: it varies by programme, and DET acceptance for Australian student visas is not guaranteed, requirements can change, and the Department of Home Affairs and individual institutions set their own policies. Verify both the US programme requirement and any Australian visa implications with the relevant authorities before choosing DET as your primary test.</p>
  <p>If your English proficiency is genuinely below the required threshold, conditional admission is a legitimate option at many US institutions. The university offers a place contingent on completing an English pathway programme first. It is a slower route, but it keeps the offer in place while you build the proficiency you need. Pathway colleges and academic English programmes exist specifically to bridge this gap, and many feed directly into undergraduate or postgraduate enrolment once language requirements are met.</p>
  <h2>Should I take IELTS or TOEFL when applying to US universities from Australia? The decision and your next step</h2>
  <p>For most Australians applying to US universities, IELTS Academic is often the stronger choice. The test aligns with Australian English conventions, Speaking is conducted with a human examiner, <a href="https://ielts.com.au/australia/test-dates" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">test dates are more flexible</a> across the country, and the preparation pathway is well-supported by structured tools and resources.</p>
  <p>The practical decision process is straightforward: check your specific programme requirements, set your target score with a 0.5 band buffer, map your test date against your application deadline, and build in one retake window. If both IELTS and TOEFL are listed, IELTS is often the more practical option for Australian applicants, unless your personal preparation history gives you a clear edge with TOEFL.</p>
  <p>The smartest first step is removing score uncertainty early. Run a diagnostic practice session on <strong>IELTS Ai Pro</strong>, see where your band score sits across all four skills, and let the platform build you a personalised study plan from there. You'll walk in knowing your band score, not guessing at it.</p>
