  <p>Here's something most IELTS instructors won't say out loud: test-takers who plateau at Band 6 are often among the most dedicated students in the room. They write essays every week. They fill notebooks with practice attempts. But without a clear picture of what an examiner actually rewards, each essay is essentially a shot in the dark. The effort is real. The direction is missing.</p>
  <p>These IELTS Writing Task 2 tips are designed to fix exactly that. Once you understand the structure behind the scoring criteria, something shifts. The essay stops being a blank-page problem and becomes a solvable puzzle with known rules. This guide walks you through that shift: how to identify the five question types, how to build a repeatable essay structure, how to manage your 40 minutes without running dry, and how to use precise vocabulary and a self-check routine tied directly to the four official band descriptors. If you want to put these strategies to work with instant, criterion-referenced feedback, <strong>IELTS Ai Pro</strong> evaluates every Task 2 essay you submit against the exact same criteria your real examiner will use.</p>
  <h2>The five Task 2 question types and why misreading them tanks your score</h2>
  <p>The single most common early mistake is treating every Task 2 prompt like a general opinion essay. It isn't. The question type determines what your essay's job actually is, and that decision has to come first, before planning, before writing, before anything else.</p>
  <h3>Recognizing the five formats quickly</h3>
  <p>The five types are: opinion (agree/disagree), discussion (both views), advantage/disadvantage, problem-solution, and double-question. Each one signals itself through specific instruction words. "To what extent do you agree or disagree?" is an opinion essay. "Discuss both views and give your own opinion" is a discussion essay. "What problems does this cause and what solutions can you suggest?" is a problem-solution essay. "What are the advantages and disadvantages?" is self-explanatory. And "Why is this happening? Do you think it is a positive development?" is a double-question: two separate prompts that both need a real answer.</p>
  <p>Once you train yourself to spot these signals, you genuinely can't unsee them. The instruction words become obvious at a glance, and the essay structure follows immediately from there. A good next step after identifying the type is to circle or underline the instruction words so they stay visible throughout your planning phase.</p>
  <h3>How the required structure changes with each question type</h3>
  <p>An opinion essay has one job: argue a consistent position. You pick a side in the introduction and every body paragraph supports that same side. A discussion essay has a different job: represent both views fairly before committing to your own. A problem-solution essay follows cause-then-fix logic, and both parts need real development. An advantage/disadvantage essay asks for balance, and if the question asks which side is greater, you have to answer that directly. A double-question essay must treat each question as its own independent task. Answering only one part, or blurring the two together, is the fastest way to lose points on Task Response.</p>
  <h2>IELTS Writing Task 2 Tips: A Repeatable Essay Structure for Any Format</h2>
  <p>Knowing the question type is step one. Knowing what to put in each paragraph is step two. The goal of having a reliable structure isn't to produce a memorized template. It's to free your brain from organizational decisions so it can focus on generating and developing actual ideas.</p>
  <h3>Writing an introduction that sets up a clear argument</h3>
  <p>A strong Task 2 introduction does exactly two things: it paraphrases the topic (never copies it word for word) and it signals your position or essay direction. Two or three sentences is the right length, a practical heuristic, not an official rule, but one that experienced teachers and published preparation guides consistently recommend. A weak introduction says something like: "Nowadays, many people think that technology is good." A stronger one paraphrases the issue and states a clear stance in the same breath. Over-long introductions eat into body paragraph time, and that's where your score actually lives.</p>
  <h3>Body paragraphs that actually develop ideas</h3>
  <p>The PEEL structure gives you a reliable paragraph engine: Point, Evidence or Explanation, Example, Link back. Published examiner guidance and band descriptor analyses consistently flag development, not topic selection, as the gap that separates Band 6 from Band 7 essays. A strong topic sentence followed by a genuinely explained reason, a concrete example, and a sentence that ties it back to the overall argument is worth far more than three thin points that never go below the surface. One well-built paragraph beats three underdeveloped ones.</p>
  <p>Here's what a Band 7 PEEL paragraph looks like in practice:</p>
  <p>> One significant advantage of remote work is the reduction in daily commuting time, which directly improves employee productivity. When workers no longer spend one to two hours traveling each day, that time can be redirected toward focused tasks or personal recovery, resulting in higher output and lower stress levels. A 2023 survey by Stanford researchers found that remote employees completed 13% more work per week than their office-based counterparts. This evidence suggests that flexible working arrangements, when well-managed, benefit both the individual and the organization as a whole.</p>
  <p>Notice what makes this paragraph work: the point is specific, the explanation develops the reasoning rather than restating it, the example is concrete, and the final sentence links back to the broader argument. That's the standard to aim for in every body paragraph.</p>
  <h3>Closing the argument without wasting your final paragraph</h3>
  <p>A conclusion has two jobs: restate your overall position and bring the essay to a logical close. The most common mistake is copying the introduction almost word for word. Instead, paraphrase your position using different phrasing and close with a broader implication sentence that shows the reader why the argument matters. New ideas in the conclusion will hurt your score, so keep this section tight and focused on what you already argued.</p>
  <h2>Managing Your 40 Minutes, IELTS Writing Task 2 Tips That Actually Work</h2>
  <p>Time management in Task 2 is a skill, and like most skills it improves with deliberate practice. Many test-takers run out of time not because they write slowly, but because they either overplan or don't plan at all, and both habits create the same result: a scramble in the final ten minutes.</p>
  <h3>The 5-30-5 time split and how to use it</h3>
  <p>The breakdown is straightforward: 5 minutes planning, 30 minutes writing, 5 minutes checking. What "planning" means in those first 5 minutes is specific. You <a href="https://magoosh.com/ielts/ielts-writing-task-2-question-types/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">identify the question type</a>, note your position or main angle, and map one clear main idea per body paragraph. That's it. The plan's purpose isn't to outline a perfect essay. It's to protect the writing phase from stalling mid-paragraph because you haven't decided what to say next.</p>
  <h3>The planning trap that quietly destroys your writing time</h3>
  <p>The trap looks like diligence: spending 12 to 15 minutes building a detailed outline because you want the essay to be structured. What actually happens is you write the first two body paragraphs under pressure and the conclusion gets three rushed sentences. The fix is a structure you've already internalized through practice. When the paragraph format is automatic, the 5-minute plan feels natural rather than rushed. Regular timed practice on a platform like <strong>IELTS Ai Pro</strong> builds this muscle. After enough sessions, the structure stops feeling like a constraint and starts feeling like a scaffold you can build on immediately.</p>
  <h2>Vocabulary and linking phrases that read naturally at Band 7+</h2>
  <p>You don't need rare, impressive words. You need the right words used with control. That distinction matters because many test-takers do the opposite: they hunt for sophisticated vocabulary and then use it incorrectly, which examiners penalize directly under Lexical Resource. Precision beats complexity.</p>
  <h3>Cohesive devices that do the job without sounding mechanical</h3>
  <p>Linking phrases organize your argument by function: addition (furthermore, in addition), contrast (however, nevertheless, on the other hand), cause and effect (therefore, as a result, consequently), example (for example, for instance, to illustrate), and conclusion (overall, in conclusion). Avoid repeating the same connector frequently, examiners notice, and it signals limited range. More importantly, natural coherence also comes from pronoun reference, clear paragraph progression, and paraphrasing key topic words, not just from inserting transition phrases. A sentence like "This policy would affect millions of families" uses "this" as a <a href="https://www.hss.iitb.ac.in/sites/default/files/2023-10/Coherence_Linking_Words%20(RMIT%20University" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">cohesive device</a>.pdf) without any formal linker at all.</p>
  <h3>Vocabulary habits that hold Band 7 essays back</h3>
  <p>The most common Lexical Resource mistakes aren't about rare words at all. They're about repeating the same word in adjacent sentences, relying on vague high-frequency words like "good," "bad," or "thing" when more precise alternatives exist, and forcing unfamiliar vocabulary into the wrong context. A practical habit: during your 5-minute planning phase, identify two or three key topic words from the prompt and think of one natural paraphrase for each. This prevents word repetition without the risk of misusing vocabulary you're uncertain about. Getting collocation right, the natural word partnerships that educated writers use instinctively, matters more at Band 7+ than vocabulary rarity.</p>
  <h2>A five-minute self-check using the official IELTS criteria</h2>
  <p>You don't have to guess what examiners want. They published exactly what they want. The <a href="https://resources.cathoven.com/ielts-writing-task-2/band-descriptors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">four official band descriptors</a> are public documents, and once you understand what each criterion means in plain language, the self-check becomes a targeted, repeatable process rather than a general re-read.</p>
  <h3>What the four official criteria mean in plain language</h3>
  <p>Task Response asks: did you answer every part of the question with a clear, fully developed position? Coherence and Cohesion asks: does the essay flow logically from sentence to sentence and paragraph to paragraph, with well-managed paragraphing? Lexical Resource asks: did you use precise, varied vocabulary without significant errors in word choice or collocation? Grammatical Range and Accuracy asks: did you use a mix of sentence structures, including complex ones, with minimal mistakes? These four criteria carry equal weight in your overall writing band score, so a strong essay needs to perform across all four, not just the ones you feel most confident about.</p>
  <h3>Questions to ask yourself in the final five minutes</h3>
  <p>Run through these after you finish writing. Did you answer every part of the prompt, including any second question? Is your position stated clearly in the introduction and consistent throughout? Did you repeat the same word several times in a short space? Are your sentences varied in length and structure, or do they all follow the same pattern? Are there obvious grammatical errors in your opening and closing paragraphs, the sections most worth polishing before time is up? These aren't questions for a printed card on exam day. They're habits to internalize through practice so the self-check becomes automatic.</p>
  <h2>How feedback turns these strategies into actual score gains</h2>
  <p>Strategies are only as powerful as the practice that follows them, and most self-study learners hit a ceiling not from lack of effort, but because their practice loop has no corrective signal built into it.</p>
  <h3>Why practicing without feedback creates blind spots</h3>
  <p>When you write an essay and judge it yourself, you tend to evaluate it by gut feel. You might sense that something is off, but you can't pinpoint whether it's a Task Response problem, a Lexical Resource gap, or a Coherence issue. As a result, the same structural habits and vocabulary errors repeat, and body paragraphs stay thin across every session. Progress stalls not from lack of effort but from the absence of criterion-referenced data on what's actually weak.</p>
  <h3>How IELTS Ai Pro maps feedback directly to your score</h3>
  <p><strong>IELTS Ai Pro</strong> evaluates every Task 2 essay you submit against all four official IELTS criteria: Task Response, Coherence and Cohesion, Lexical Resource, and Grammatical Range and Accuracy. The feedback includes inline corrections and specific next-step recommendations, so every practice essay returns a detailed report rather than just a band estimate. That means you can apply every IELTS Writing Task 2 tip covered in this article and immediately see which criteria improved and which still need work. The platform's adaptive study path then prioritizes your next practice activities based on that data, so your time goes toward what actually moves your score rather than what feels comfortable to practice.</p>
  <h2>The difference is knowing what to aim for</h2>
  <p>The gap between Band 6 and Band 7+ is rarely about intelligence or effort. It's about knowing exactly what the examiner is looking for and building the habits to deliver it consistently. The loop this article builds is straightforward: identify the question type, follow a <a href="https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/ielts-writing-task-2-essay-structure-band-7" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">repeatable structure</a>, manage your 40 minutes with intention, and self-check against the four criteria before you put your pen down, with precise, varied language carrying you through every step.</p>
  <p>Follow these IELTS Writing Task 2 tips and pair them with consistent, <a href="https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/ielts-writing-feedback-rewrite-method" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">feedback-driven practice</a> and your score will reflect the work. Reading about PEEL structure or cohesive devices doesn't move your band. Writing essays and seeing exactly where they fall short does. Start with a <a href="https://www.ieltsai.pro/ielts-writing-checker" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">free practice essay</a> on <strong>IELTS Ai Pro</strong>, get instant criterion-referenced feedback, and find out exactly where your next improvement is hiding.</p>
  <p><strong>Related practice:</strong> <a href="/ielts-writing-checker">IELTS Writing checker</a> and <a href="/blog/ielts-writing-task-2-essay-structure-band-7">Band 7 Task 2 structure guide</a>.</p>
