  <p>You've done the practice. You've submitted essay after essay. And you're still landing at the same band. That experience is more common than most test-takers realise, and the reason is rarely a lack of effort. The most reliable way to improve your IELTS essay score is to treat it as a diagnostic problem first, and a practice volume problem second.</p>
  <p>IELTS examiners score Writing Task 2 against four specific criteria: Task Response, Coherence and Cohesion, Lexical Resource, and Grammatical Range and Accuracy. All four criteria are considered in the Writing assessment, so if one is quietly dragging your score down, practising harder on the others won't close the gap. Once you understand what each criterion demands at band 6, 7, and 8, the path to improvement becomes concrete rather than guesswork. Platforms like <strong>IELTS Ai Pro</strong> score practice essays against those exact four criteria and return per-criterion feedback instantly, but the first step is understanding the framework itself.</p>
  <h2>The four criteria that actually determine your writing band</h2>
  <h3>Task Achievement: answering exactly what was asked</h3>
  <p>Band 6 and band 7 both require you to "address all parts of the task," but the key difference is depth and consistency. At band 6, some parts of the prompt are covered more fully than others, and the position can become unclear or repetitive by the conclusion. At band 7, you present a clear position throughout the entire essay, not just in the introduction. The most common failure here is answering a general version of the question rather than the specific prompt. If the question asks whether governments or individuals should reduce carbon emissions, writing about the environment in general terms will not score above band 6.</p>
  <h3>Coherence and Cohesion: structure the reader barely notices</h3>
  <p>These two terms describe different things. Coherence refers to the logical flow of your argument. Cohesion refers to the linking devices that connect sentences and paragraphs. At band 6, cohesion is often mechanical: every sentence opens with "Furthermore" or "On the other hand" regardless of whether those connectors fit the logic. At band 7, paragraphing is purposeful, with one central idea per paragraph and clear progression between them. The examiner phrase "organisation is present but mechanical" captures the band 6 pattern precisely.</p>
  <h3>Lexical Resource and Grammatical Range: precision over complexity</h3>
  <p>The persistent myth is that harder words or longer sentences automatically lift these scores. They don't. Examiners reward <a href="https://ielts5to6.com/grammar/ielts-writing-task-2-collocations-list-pdf-friendly/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">accurate collocations</a> and vocabulary used with precision, not vocabulary used aspirationally. A sophisticated word used incorrectly does more damage than a simpler word used precisely. The same logic applies to grammar: a variety of complex structures is rewarded only when those structures are controlled. According to the <a href="https://ieltsimmigration.ir/wp-content/uploads/2020/03/Band-Descriptors-Task-2.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official band descriptors</a>, examiners value accuracy in complex structures over the mere attempt at complexity, a well-constructed complex sentence demonstrates more control than several poorly managed ones.</p>
  <h2>Where most band 5 and 6 essays lose marks</h2>
  <h3>The band 5 essay: partially addressed, position unclear</h3>
  <p>The official descriptor is "addresses the task only partially," and it shows up in a predictable pattern. The writer answers a version of the prompt but misses one of its parts. The position is implied rather than stated. Paragraphing is weak or absent, so ideas blend together. Vocabulary is repetitive because the writer lacks the lexical flexibility to vary expressions, and grammar errors are frequent enough to occasionally affect meaning.</p>
  <h3>The band 6 essay: addressed but underdeveloped</h3>
  <p>A band 6 writer does answer the question, which puts them ahead of band 5. The problem is that ideas are stated rather than developed. A body paragraph might open with a relevant point, but instead of explaining or substantiating it, the writer moves on. Body paragraphs often contain more than one main idea, which scatters the argument and simultaneously weakens Coherence and Task Response. Cohesive devices are overused or mechanical, that same descriptor, structure without purpose, applies directly here.</p>
  <h3>The shift from band 6 to band 7</h3>
  <p>This is where most serious test-takers get stuck, and the fix is specific. The jump from 6 to 7 is not about adding more words or more linking phrases. It comes down to three targeted adjustments: developing each idea fully within its paragraph rather than listing multiple ideas; maintaining a consistent position throughout the essay; and controlling vocabulary accurately rather than attempting advanced words without confidence. A band 7 essay with straightforward vocabulary used precisely will outscore a band 6 essay scattered with misused sophisticated terms, consistently and measurably.</p>
  <h2>How to improve your IELTS essay score in 40 minutes</h2>
  <h3>Phase 1 (minutes 0, 7): read, plan, and map your argument</h3>
  <p>This is the most neglected phase in exam conditions. Read the question twice: on the first pass, identify the topic; on the second, identify every part of the prompt and the specific task type. Then write a four-point outline before drafting a single sentence. Note your thesis, two body paragraph ideas, and one specific support point for each. This seven-minute investment protects Task Achievement and Coherence before the pen moves. It also prevents the common mistake of realising halfway through that you've answered the wrong question. Decide your position clearly before writing. An ambiguous stance at this stage almost always produces an unclear essay.</p>
  <h3>Phase 2 (minutes 7, 35): writing with structure and intent</h3>
  <p>Open with a clear thesis that states your position, not a restatement of the question prompt. Each body paragraph gets one central idea, a clear explanation, and a specific example or piece of reasoning. Your conclusion restates the position in different words and summarises the two main points without introducing new arguments. Use the final five minutes for two targeted proofreading checks: first, scan for verb tense consistency, article use, and subject-verb agreement; second, identify repeated vocabulary and replace it with a more precise or varied alternative. These two checks alone can remove the low-hanging errors that examiners flag under Grammatical Range and Lexical Resource, and can shift a mid-band 6 essay meaningfully upward.</p>
  <h2>What band 6, 7 and 8 essays actually look like</h2>
  <h3>Band 6 vs. band 7: where the argument deepens</h3>
  <p>Using a sample prompt about entertainers' pay, the band 6 essay states a position and provides relevant points, but relies on broad generalisations. The writer notes that "entertainers can bring happiness to society." The band 7 writer develops that same idea with specific reasoning: "when celebrities support environmental campaigns, they draw attention to important issues far more effectively than ordinary individuals can." The vocabulary shift is equally telling. "Happiness" becomes "emotional relief." "Bad message" becomes "superficial values." The idea hasn't changed; the depth of reasoning and the precision of language have. That is the <a href="https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/ielts-writing-task-2-essay-structure-band-7" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">band 6 to band 7 transition</a> in practice.</p>
  <h3>Band 8: what "nuanced position" and "measurable impact" look like</h3>
  <p>The band 8 essay on the same prompt doesn't just argue for or against. It qualifies the argument, contending that entertainers deserve their income "not because fame itself is valuable, but because entertainment generates emotional, cultural, and economic benefits." What distinguishes this response is that each paragraph is tightly developed with specific reasoning rather than broad statements. Phrases like "distorts social priorities" and "measurable public impact" replace the generalisations that anchor lower-band responses. The difference is not vocabulary complexity. It is reasoning quality, and the two are not the same thing.</p>
  <h2>Improve IELTS essay score: vocabulary and grammar moves that lift your lexical band</h2>
  <h3>Topic collocations worth learning by theme</h3>
  <p>Strong Lexical Resource comes from accurate collocations, not rare words. The most transferable collocations for Task 2 span four recurring themes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Education:</strong> academic attainment, curriculum reform, educational inequality, critical thinking skills</li>
    <li><strong>Environment:</strong> carbon emissions, sustainable development, fossil fuel dependency, net-zero targets</li>
    <li><strong>Health:</strong> preventive measures, diet-related illness, public health system, mental health support</li>
    <li><strong>Society:</strong> social cohesion, income gap, government intervention, community welfare</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These collocations recur across Task 2 prompts and align with the high-frequency topic areas identified in official IELTS preparation guidance. Examiners reward accurate collocations used in the right context over sophisticated single words dropped incorrectly into sentences.</p>
  <h3>Linking phrases and argument verbs that signal band 7 and above</h3>
  <p>At band 6, cohesion often means "Furthermore" and "On the other hand" repeated throughout the essay. At band 7 and above, linking is more selective and reflects the actual logical relationship between ideas. Where a connector needs to signal contrast, consider precise alternatives such as "Conversely" or "Nonetheless" when the relationship genuinely calls for them, rather than defaulting to a mechanical "However." "Consequently" or "As a result" signal cause and effect more naturally than "So." Argument verbs are equally useful for academic register: "advocate," "contend," "concede," and "refute" allow you to present and engage with arguments without requiring complex sentence construction. Used accurately, they signal the analytical thinking that examiners associate with band 7 performance and above.</p>
  <h2>Why generic feedback won't move your band, and what to do instead</h2>
  <h3>The feedback gap most self-study learners hit</h3>
  <p>Practising essays without <a href="https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/ielts-writing-feedback-rewrite-method" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">criterion-referenced feedback</a> is like training for a fitness test without measuring any of your results. You write. You improve vaguely. But you have no way of knowing whether your Task Response is at 6.5 while your Lexical Resource sits at 5.5, because nobody has told you. A friend's review, a grammar checker, or general tutor feedback won't break down performance by criterion. Because all four criteria are considered in the Writing assessment, a consistently low score in one area will limit your overall band regardless of how strong the others are.</p>
  <h3>How IELTS Ai Pro gives you criterion-aligned scores on every practice essay</h3>
  <p><strong><a href="https://www.ieltsai.pro/ielts-writing-checker" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IELTS Ai Pro</a></strong> is an AI-powered platform designed to evaluate Task 2 practice essays against all four official IELTS criteria, returning an <a href="https://ielts.idp.com/results/scores/writing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">estimated band score per criterion</a>, inline corrections, and specific next-step recommendations after every submission. Instead of a vague comment like "try to vary your vocabulary," you can see which criterion is holding back your band score and why. The platform offers an entry-level option so you can explore criterion-aligned AI writing feedback and identify your weakest area before committing to a paid plan. Every practice session becomes purposeful rather than repetitive, because you know precisely where to direct the next hour of study.</p>
  <h2>Putting it all together</h2>
  <p>IELTS Writing Task 2 improvement is a diagnostic problem before it is a practice volume problem. If you don't know which criterion is capping your score, writing more essays won't fix it. These IELTS essay tips apply whether you're aiming to boost your IELTS band from 5 to 6 or push through the 6-to-7 threshold that most test-takers find hardest to cross.</p>
  <p>Apply the four-criteria framework as a lens to every essay you write from here. Use <a href="https://careerwiseenglish.com.au/ielts-writing-task-2-time-management/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the 40-minute routine</a> to protect each criterion in exam conditions. Measure your performance against model band 6, 7, and 8 essays on the same prompt to understand what "well-developed" and "precise vocabulary" actually look like in practice, not just in theory.</p>
  <p>The clearest way to improve your IELTS essay score reliably is to apply the four-criteria framework and the 40-minute routine with diagnostic intent. Submit a practice essay on <strong>IELTS Ai Pro</strong>, see where each of the four criteria sits, and focus your next session on the one pulling the average down. That is how bands move.</p>
  <p><strong>Related practice:</strong> <a href="/ielts-writing-checker">IELTS Writing checker</a> and <a href="/blog/ielts-writing-task-2-examples">IELTS Writing Task 2 examples</a>.</p>
