  <p>I want to tell you about a frustrating week I had a few years into teaching IELTS writing, and into watching students struggle to improve their IELTS writing score. A student came to me having written three full practice essays. She had planned carefully, used a template, varied her vocabulary. And the feedback still said Band 6. She looked at me and asked the question I couldn't shake: "What am I actually doing wrong?"</p>
  <p>That question stuck because it's the real one. Most people who are stuck at Band 6 are not stuck because they're lazy. They're stuck because the feedback they receive doesn't connect the score to a specific, fixable problem. Generic advice like "develop your ideas more" or "use better vocabulary" sounds helpful but leaves the gap exactly where it was. This article takes a different approach.</p>
  <p>What follows walks through the real mechanics behind each of the four official scoring criteria, shows you what a weak paragraph looks like next to a stronger one, and lays out an 8-week plan you can start this week. Here are practical IELTS writing tips to help raise your IELTS writing score by one full band. One band of improvement is not a fantasy, it's a realistic outcome of targeted, deliberate practice with the right feedback attached to each attempt.</p>
  <h2>What the four IELTS writing criteria actually measure</h2>
  <p>Before you can fix your score, you need to understand exactly what examiners are evaluating. The four criteria are Task Achievement (Task 1) or Task Response (Task 2), Coherence and Cohesion, Lexical Resource, and Grammatical Range and Accuracy. They sound familiar, but most test-takers have only a surface-level understanding of what each one actually rewards.</p>
  <h3>Task Achievement vs. Task Response: why the difference matters</h3>
  <p>Task 1 is scored on Task Achievement, which means examiners are looking for accurate coverage of the key features in the data, a clear overview, and appropriate data selection. Task 2, by contrast, is scored on Task Response, which asks something quite different: Did you directly address all parts of the question? Do you have a clear, consistent position? Are your ideas extended and supported? Most writers treat both tasks the same way, building a template and filling it in. That instinct costs marks, because the two tasks are fundamentally asking for different things.</p>
  <p><strong>Task 2 is worth twice as much as Task 1 in the final score calculation.</strong> This means a weak Task 2 response can pull your entire writing band down even if your Task 1 is clean and well-organized. Most test-takers do not factor this in when they decide how to divide their energy and study time.</p>
  <h3>Where Band 5 and Band 6 writers consistently lose points</h3>
  <p>At Band 5, ideas are present but barely developed. At Band 6, ideas are relevant but stay general. The official descriptors describe Band 6 Task Response as "addresses the task, though some parts may be more fully developed than others." That phrase, "some parts," is where most of the lost marks live. The jump to Band 7 is not about writing more sentences. It's about going deeper with each idea and maintaining that depth across all four criteria at the same time.</p>
  <h2>How to improve your IELTS writing score: Task 2 fixes that actually work</h2>
  <p>Band 6 writers tend to make the same handful of mistakes. Recognizing those patterns in your own writing, with a concrete example to compare against, is usually the turning point. The fixes below target the specific gaps the IELTS writing band descriptors identify between Band 6 and Band 7.</p>
  <h3>Before and after: a Band 6 paragraph rewritten at Band 7</h3>
  <p>Here is a Band 6 body paragraph on the topic of free university education:</p>
  <p>*"One reason people support free university education is that it helps students. If education is free, more people can study without worrying about money. This is good because many families cannot afford high tuition fees. For example, some students may decide to go to university instead of working."*</p>
  <p>Now here is the same idea written at Band 7:</p>
  <p>*"One major advantage of free university education is that it can improve social equality by widening access to higher learning. When tuition fees are removed, students from low-income families are no longer excluded simply because of cost, giving them a fairer opportunity to obtain qualifications and secure better employment. Evidence from countries such as Germany and Sweden suggests that heavily subsidized higher education correlates with higher degree completion rates among students from disadvantaged backgrounds."*</p>
  <p>The topic is identical. What changed? The Band 7 version has a focused topic sentence, explains the mechanism (removing fees widens access, which improves equality), and uses a concrete example with specific detail. The vocabulary is more precise without feeling forced. And the paragraph stays on one idea instead of drifting. <strong>That combination of depth, mechanism, and precision is what separates Band 6 from Band 7.</strong></p>
  <h3>Two patterns that quietly cap essays below Band 7</h3>
  <p>The first is not addressing all parts of the question prompt. Many writers read the question quickly and respond to the general theme, missing a secondary question buried in the prompt. Examiners notice every part of the prompt and score accordingly. The second pattern is giving an example without explaining how it supports the argument. Stating "for example, Japan has an aging population" is not enough, you need to show why that example proves your point. Both mistakes share the same root cause: moving too fast from claim to conclusion without building the logical bridge in between. That bridge is exactly what the Task Response criterion rewards.</p>
  <p>A third issue worth calling out separately is positional drift: stating a clear argument in the introduction and then quietly softening or contradicting it in the body paragraphs. Individual sentences can be grammatically fine and still produce an incoherent essay when the underlying position keeps shifting.</p>
  <h2>How to write with coherence, not just linking words</h2>
  <p>A lot of test-takers believe coherence means scattering "furthermore," "however," and "in addition" throughout their essays. That is not coherence. Those words are decorations. Real coherence is the logical architecture that allows an examiner to follow your argument from sentence to sentence without needing to re-read anything.</p>
  <h3>What coherence looks like at Band 7 and above</h3>
  <p>At Band 7, each paragraph has one central idea, a developed explanation, and a clear connection back to the essay's main position. The official descriptor says Band 7 writing "logically organises information and ideas" with "clear progression throughout." The step up from Band 6, where progression is described as "clear overall" but cohesion may be "faulty or mechanical," is about making that logical thread visible inside each paragraph, not just between them.</p>
  <h3>Practical techniques for better paragraph flow</h3>
  <p>Start with topic sentences that state the paragraph's specific claim rather than restating or echoing the essay question. That distinction alone resolves a large share of the "faulty or mechanical" cohesion the Band 6 descriptor identifies. From there, use reference words ("this approach," "these factors," "such policies") instead of repeating noun phrases in consecutive sentences, repeated nouns are one of the clearest signals of mechanical writing. Finally, close each body paragraph with a sentence that ties the example back to your thesis. That closing sentence is where Band 6 writers most often stop early, and it is exactly the kind of "clear progression" the Band 7 descriptor rewards.</p>
  <h2>Building vocabulary that impresses examiners without sounding forced</h2>
  <p>The Lexical Resource criterion is not a competition to see how many rare words you can use. It rewards flexibility and accuracy. A word that is "advanced" but misused does more damage to your score than a simpler word used precisely.</p>
  <h3>What precision actually means in practice</h3>
  <p><a href="https://blog.myieltsclassroom.com/5-common-ielts-lexical-resource-mistakes/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Collocation errors</a> are one of the most frequent score-lowering mistakes in this criterion. Phrases like "make damage," "do a crime," or "rise awareness" signal a lack of control even when the surrounding vocabulary is strong. The correct collocations, "cause damage," "commit a crime," "raise awareness", show familiarity with how words actually work together in English. <strong>Precision means knowing not just the word but the company it keeps.</strong> Over-paraphrasing, replacing a clear phrase with an inaccurate synonym because it looks more advanced, is an equally common trap.</p>
  <h3>Daily IELTS writing habits to improve your score through vocabulary</h3>
  <p>Three practices make the most difference over 8 weeks. First, learn new words in sentence context, not in isolation. A definition tells you what a word means; a sentence shows you how it behaves. Second, use topic-based vocabulary in timed writing sessions so those words become retrievable under exam pressure, not just recognizable on a flashcard. Third, note the collocation, word family, and register alongside each new word. "Substantial" is academic; "huge" is conversational. Knowing which context calls for which word is what the criterion actually measures. IELTS Ai Pro's vocabulary tools are designed around this approach: topic-based word lists, flashcards, and strategy guides aligned with the themes that appear on actual IELTS exams, so the vocabulary you're learning is always relevant to the writing prompts you'll face.</p>
  <h2>Managing 60 minutes so both tasks get the attention they deserve</h2>
  <p><a href="https://careerwiseenglish.com.au/ielts-writing-task-2-time-management/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Time management</a> is one of the few areas where a single behavioral change can deliver immediate results in your next practice session. The most common pattern among stuck writers is either spending too long on Task 1 or skipping the planning phase for Task 2 entirely.</p>
  <h3>Task 1: how to use 20 minutes without cutting corners</h3>
  <p>The recommended split is 3 minutes analyzing the prompt and identifying key features, 13 to 14 minutes writing, and 3 minutes proofreading. The overview sentence deserves a real portion of that planning time. It is the single biggest Task Achievement marker in Task 1, and a missing or vague overview immediately caps your score. Writers who rush straight into describing data often produce a response that reads like a list of details without a controlling observation, which is precisely what Band 6 Task Achievement looks like in the descriptors.</p>
  <h3>Task 2: front-loading the plan to write faster</h3>
  <p>For Task 2, use the 5-30-5 split: 5 minutes planning, 30 minutes writing, 5 minutes checking. Writers who plan well write faster because they are not stalling mid-paragraph to figure out what comes next. A simple planning framework covers four things: the question type, your position, two supporting ideas, and one counterpoint or concession. That five-minute investment means the 30 minutes of writing stay focused and directional rather than exploratory.</p>
  <h2>An 8-week practice plan that targets one band of improvement</h2>
  <p>Improvement comes from deliberate practice with feedback attached, not from writing one essay after another and hoping the score climbs on its own. Each phase in this plan has a specific focus so the work stays targeted.</p>
  <h3>Weeks 1, 2: establish your baseline and understand the criteria</h3>
  <p>Start by writing a timed Task 1 and Task 2 under exam conditions. Then read the <a href="https://takeielts.britishcouncil.org/sites/default/files/ielts_writing_band_descriptors.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Band 6 and Band 7 descriptors</a> side by side for each of the four criteria and score your own writing as honestly as you can. Identify the criterion where you are furthest from Band 7. That becomes the priority for the next phase. Most writers can identify their weakest area clearly once they read the descriptors carefully, because the gap between "cohesion may be faulty or mechanical" and "uses a range of cohesive devices appropriately" becomes visible in their own writing once they know what to look for.</p>
  <h3>Weeks 3, 6: deliberate practice with criterion-based feedback</h3>
  <p>Write 2 to 3 timed tasks per week, alternating Task 1 and Task 2. Each week, focus on one criterion in depth: Task Response in week 3, Coherence and Cohesion in week 4, Lexical Resource in week 5, and <a href="https://www.ieltsfreetests.com/learn-ielts/writing/ielts-grammar-for-writing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Grammar in week 6</a>. This is where the quality of your feedback becomes the limiting factor. <strong>IELTS Ai Pro provides instant AI feedback that evaluates all four criteria with inline corrections and specific next-step recommendations tied directly to the band descriptors.</strong> For writers without access to a tutor, this closes the feedback loop that would otherwise stay open until exam day. Submit a Task 2 essay and know within minutes which criterion is holding you back and why, rather than guessing.</p>
  <h3>Weeks 7, 8: exam-condition simulation and consolidation</h3>
  <p>Do at least one full timed writing session per week under real exam conditions: no pausing, no phone, no dictionary. Review your <a href="https://www.ieltsai.pro/blog/ielts-reading-band-6-5-error-log" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">error log</a> and identify patterns that haven't resolved yet. Rewrite at least one weak essay in full, not just correcting individual sentences but rebuilding the paragraph architecture. The goal in these two weeks is not to learn new things but to consolidate the changes you've already made so they become automatic under pressure.</p>
  <h2>The gap between effort and score is almost always fixable</h2>
  <p>Think back to the student who practiced all week and still saw Band 6. The effort was real. The problem was that nobody had connected the score to a specific, named criterion with a specific, fixable pattern. Once that connection was made, the path forward became clear.</p>
  <p>The core message here is straightforward: know what the criteria actually reward, identify the specific patterns keeping your score at 6, and practice with feedback that names both the criterion and the fix. Manage your time deliberately so both tasks get the attention the scoring weights demand. With targeted practice and the right feedback, you can improve your IELTS writing score by one band in eight weeks, and that outcome is standard, not optimistic, when the feedback loop is closed.</p>
  <p>The first step costs 40 minutes: write a timed Task 2 under exam conditions, then read the Band 6 and Band 7 descriptors side by side and score yourself honestly. See which criterion creates the most distance. If you want that diagnosis done instantly, with inline corrections and band-specific recommendations, IELTS Ai Pro's AI writing evaluation does exactly that on your first practice essay, free. The gap between effort and score is almost always smaller than it feels once you know where to look.</p>
